}
``

Hmm wait — but if the release-append FAILS (best-effort catch), clearing loses the pending release. Acceptable tradeoff? The release is a no-op hygiene event (releasing ids that may not be pinned). If the pin=true for ster2 succeeded, ster1's unpin already happened... Actually the release happens BEFORE the pin in the same cycle. If release-append fails but pin-append succeeds: ster1 stays pinned forever (stale steering pinned + newest pinned = 2 steering pinned — violates "one steering pinned"). Edge case; but retrying the release on the next cycle would fix it. Alternative: keep release until the NEXT pin succeeds — complexity. Middle ground: clear the release only after the pin append succeeded (both in the same try). If the try throws, keep release for retry AND keep pins pending... but takeForFlush already consumed pins. The failure window is tiny (append failures are disk errors); per best-effort contract, accept.

Also the trailing events show ANOTHER issue: the final flush cycles keep releasing 93adb079 — with the fix, after the pin(true [93adb079]) cycle, pinnedSteringRecordIds=[93adb079]; the NEXT cycle releases it (no new pins) — a stray unpin! That's WORSE: a release with no replacement pin would UNPIN the newest steering entirely!

The release must only fire when a NEW steering pin lands (release-then-pin pair), not standalone. So: takeForFlush returns release ONLY alongside steeringPin:

```dart
if (steringPin.isEmpty) return (release: const [], pin: [...promptPin]);
final release = pinnedSteringRecordIds;
pinnedSteringRecordIds = steeringPin;
return (release: release, pin: [...promptPin, ...steringPin]);
``

The earlier debug (before my state fix) showed standalone releases because pinnedSteringRecordIds was set without pins landing. With the corrected state machine, standalone releases never make sense.

